<div xmlns:perseus="http://www.perseus.org/meta/perseus.rdfs#" xmlns:dctype="http://purl.org/dc/dcmitype/" xmlns:tufts="http://www.tufts.edu/" xmlns:alph="http://alpheios.net/namespaces/tei" class="alpheios-lex-text_container en">
    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n31191" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">nōnus</span></b> , a, um, <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b><i>adj. ord.</i> [for
			novenus, from novem], <i>the ninth</i>: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">terra nona,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Cic.
					Rep. 6, 18, 18</span></i>
			: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">accedes opera agro nona Sabino,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Hor.
					S. 2, 7, 118</span></i>
			.—</div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>II.</b>
			<i>Subst.</i>: <b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">nōna</span></b> , ae, f.
		</div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense2"><b>A.</b> (Sc. hora.) <i>The ninth hour of the day</i>, i. e. the third
			before sunset, at which hour business was ended at Rome: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">post nonam venies,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Hor. Ep. 1, 7, 71</span></i>
			; <i><span class="en">Mart. 4, 8, 5</span></i>.—</div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense2"><b>B.</b> (Sc. pars.) <i>The ninth
				part</i>: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">nonas praedae vovere,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Just.
					20, 3, 3</span></i>
			.—Hence, adv.: <b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">nōnō</span></b> , <i>ninthly</i>, <i><span class="en">Cassiod. de Anim.
			12</span></i>.</div>
    </div> <div class="alpheios-lex-footnotes en"> </div>
    <div class="alpheios-lex-alph-source">From <u>
            <u>A Latin Dictionary</u>
        </u> (Charlton T. Lewis, Charles Short, Oxford, Clarendon Press, 1879) </div>
